Thanks for the clear report — this is expected behavior in the sense that relying on \:… as a literal “colon+token” in an Express route is fragile.

Short summary / cause

Express route parsing is powered by path-to-regexp. That library does not reliably support escaping a : to make it behave as a literal colon in the route pattern, and people have reported that “escaping the colon” doesn’t work as expected. 
[GitHub](https://github.com/pillarjs/path-to-regexp/discussions/274?utm_source=chatgpt.com)

In practice the observed GET / POST difference is most likely due to differences in how clients/transport encode the path (or how the raw request URL is presented to the router) rather than a deterministic “HTTP method changes route parsing” rule inside Express. Because the escaping/encoding is not standardized for this case, you can’t depend on consistent behavior across clients/requests. (See Express routing docs for how route patterns and : parameters are intended to be used.) 
expressjs.com
+1

Reproducible test (suggestion)
Run these two requests (replace :bookId with a concrete value like 123):

# GET (example)
curl -v "http://localhost:3000/books/123:upVote"

# POST (example)
curl -v -X POST "http://localhost:3000/books/123:upVote"


If you want a more explicit test that isolates client-side encoding differences, percent-encode the ::

curl -v "http://localhost:3000/books/123%3AupVote"
curl -v -X POST "http://localhost:3000/books/123%3AupVote"


If the encoded form (%3A) behaves consistently, that confirms the mismatch is due to how the literal : is being encoded/seen by the server.

Recommendations / workarounds

Avoid literal : in path names. If you control the API shape, prefer a different, unambiguous path:

POST /books/:bookId/upVote or POST /books/:bookId/up-vote — easier and idiomatic for REST.

Use percent-encoding on the client. If you must use a literal :upVote suffix, ensure clients send %3AupVote in the path (i.e. encodeURIComponent the path segment). That tends to give consistent behavior because the router sees the encoded character explicitly.

Match-and-strip fallback inside a single route. If you can’t change clients, use a single route that captures the whole trailing segment and split it in your handler:

app.all('/books/:bookSegment', (req, res, next) => {
  // bookSegment might be '123:upVote' or '123'
  const raw = req.params.bookSegment;
  const bookId = raw.replace(/:upVote$/, '');
  // continue handling by method
  if (req.method === 'POST' && raw.endsWith(':upVote')) {
    // handle upVote
  } else {
    // normal handling
  }
});


Use query param or header for the “custom method” (e.g. /books/:bookId?custom=upVote or X-Custom-Method: upVote) — avoids path parsing headaches altogether.

Why this is unlikely to be fixed by “escaping”

Multiple discussions and issues indicate path-to-regexp and many routing implementations don’t treat \: as a stable way to match a literal colon in the route syntax — escaping a colon is not a supported/portable route pattern. For that reason, escaping-based approaches are fragile and not reliable across versions/clients. 
