Commander takes the approach that if an option is specified multiple times, the last one wins. This is fairly common in CLI tools.

The original design of the custom option parsing is that it transforms a value. So it does not get called when the option is used without an option-argument. Which is sometimes annoying when it would be useful!

The way you can get a callback in this situation is with a custom event listener. These events are to some extent a historical artefact of the implementation and only lightly documented. There are newer better ways of doing some of the things these were used for, but there isn't another way to catch multiple uses of an option with an optional argument.

https://github.com/tj/commander.js#custom-event-listeners

```javascript
program.on('option:verbose', function () {
  process.env.VERBOSE = this.opts().verbose;
});
```

