Unfortunately, this is a fundamental TypeScript limitation, not an SWR design issue. TypeScript can only narrow types based on compile-time type information. When suspense has type `boolean`, there's no way for TypeScript to know at compile time whether it will be true or false at runtime.

However you can preserve the literal type through generics.

You have to do it like this

```ts
export const useUser = <T extends boolean>(suspense: T) => {
  return useSWR("Key", () => { return new Promise<string>((resolve) => setTimeout(() => resolve("Hello world"), 2500)) }, { suspense })
}
```

`onSuccess` is designed to be just a _callback_ after triggering the mutation, not an _intermediate step_ during the mutation. And you'll have to handle the life cycle and errors yourself:

